  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nA postively-tilted trough will track across the middle Ohio\nValley and Appalachians today, bringing scattered showers and \nthunderstorms along with it. GOES satellite imagery shows clouds\nbreaking for brief pockets of sunshine across the middle Ohio \nValley, and this daytime heating may provide fuel for isolated \nstronger thunderstorms this afternoon and evening. However, the \nthick cloud cover has likely limited the overall severe \nthunderstorm threat today. If a few storms are able to mature, \ndeep-layer shear will be just enough (30-40 kts) to support \norganized convection. SPC mesoanalysis also shows around 100 \nm2/s2 of 0-1 km storm relative helicity across central and \neastern portions of West Virginia today, which could provide \nsupport for a brief spin-up tornado. However, the main threat of\nany storms that mature today would be localized damaging wind \ngusts and cloud-to-ground lightning.\n\nIn addition to a marginal severe weather threat, there is also a \nthreat of flash flooding due to a very saturated airmass. \nPrecipitable water values are highest today across central, \nnorthern, and eastern West Virginia at 2.00 to 2.25 inches. These \nare the locations where flooding will be a concern today, and a \nFlood Watch remains in effect for these areas until late tonight. \nAny place that sees training showers and thunderstorms today will be \nat risk of flash flooding.\n\nThe aforementioned trough will escape to the northeast Monday \nand Tuesday, but a cutoff upper-level low will develop over the \nSoutheast on Tuesday and hang around through much of the \nworkweek. The upper-level low and lingering vorticity aloft will\nsupport the development of showers and thunderstorms each day \nthis week, with the highest chances occurring in the afternoon \nand evening hours during peak heating.",
